Approach
I use a sliding window because all array values are positive.

I maintain:

left = start of the current window
sum = sum of the current window
best[i] = shortest target-sum sub-array found in arr[0...i]
answer = minimum total length of two non-overlapping target-sum sub-arrays
I move right from left to right and add arr[right] to sum.

If sum > target, I move left forward and subtract values from the window until sum <= target.

When sum == target, I found a valid sub-array:

arr = [3, 2, 4, 3]
target = 3

index:   0   1   2   3
arr:    [3,  2,  4,  3]

First target sum:
          [3]
           ↑
        length = 1

Second target sum:
                   [3]
                    ↑
                 length = 1

prefix best:
index:      0   1   2   3
best:       1   1   1   1

At index 3:
current sub-array = [3]
current length    = 1

previous valid part = best[2] = 1

total = 1 + 1 = 2

The important part is best[left - 1].

For the second [3], left = 3, so I look at best[2]. That guarantees that the previous sub-array finishes at index 2 or earlier, so the two sub-arrays cannot overlap.

After processing the current position, I update:

best[right] = min(best[right - 1], currentLength)
This means best[right] always stores the shortest valid target-sum sub-array seen anywhere up to right.

If there is no valid previous sub-array, I simply do not create a candidate.

At the end, if I never found two valid non-overlapping sub-arrays, I return -1.

CODE:-

class Solution {
public:
    int minSumOfLengths(vector<int>& arr, int target) {
        int n = arr.size();

        vector<int> best(n, INT_MAX);

        int left = 0;
        int sum = 0;
        int answer = INT_MAX;

        for (int right = 0; right < n; ++right) {
            sum += arr[right];

            while (sum > target) {
                sum -= arr[left];
                ++left;
            }

            if (sum == target) {
                int currentLength = right - left + 1;

                if (left > 0 && best[left - 1] != INT_MAX) {
                    answer = min(answer,
                                 currentLength + best[left - 1]);
                }

                best[right] = currentLength;
            }

            if (right > 0) {
                best[right] = min(best[right], best[right - 1]);
            }
        }

        return answer == INT_MAX ? -1 : answer;
    }
};
